"""Post-process fc/content.json (from impl_fc.py) into the multi-level FreshCheck (v2) manuscript."""
import json
P='/home/claude/fc/content.json'; C=json.load(open(P))
def rep(prefix, new, key='text'):
    hits=[b for b in C if b.get(key,'').startswith(prefix)]; assert len(hits)==1,(prefix,len(hits)); hits[0][key]=new
def sub(prefix, a, b, key='text'):
    hits=[x for x in C if x.get(key,'').startswith(prefix)]; assert len(hits)==1 and a in hits[0][key],(prefix,a[:30]); hits[0][key]=hits[0][key].replace(a,b)
rep('A leakage-aware image classifier', 'A leakage-aware multi-level image classifier for fresh and spoiled food with a refer-to-staff option: development and session-grouped validation on public datasets')
rep('This study develops and validates FreshCheck',
"This study develops and validates FreshCheck, an image-based classifier that labels food as fresh or spoiled and refers uncertain images to staff. FreshCheck averages the class probabilities of nine logistic-regression models built on colour and texture features, frozen MobileNetV3 features and frozen ResNet-50 features taken at three depths from whole-image and local views, and refers an image when its highest probability falls below a threshold set on training data so that at most 1% of spoiled images are accepted as fresh. It was evaluated on public red-meat (2,266 images), chicken-breast (1,300) and fruit (1,894) datasets with capture-session-grouped cross-validation, after screening each dataset for duplicate images. Screening showed that consecutive red-meat files were frames of one session and that 491 of 500 'good' files in a paneer-curry set were copies of nine photographs; image-level splits therefore overstated accuracy (red meat, final-layer features: 98.4% versus 86.9% with sessions held out; p = 0.048). Under session-grouped validation FreshCheck reached 95.2% on red meat, 98.4% on chicken and 97.4% on fruit, against 90.3%, 97.2% and 97.2% for fusion of final-layer features; mid-level features accounted for most of the gain. On chicken the refer rule decided 96.8% of images with 99.3% accuracy and lowered spoiled samples accepted as fresh from 1.8% to 0.8%. Models moved to the other meat dataset without local images failed (49–67%); about 80 to 170 labelled local images restored 94–97%. A demonstration application and all code are released. Image-based freshness checks should be validated by session and calibrated with local images.")
sub('Our aim was to develop', "(iii) build FreshCheck by late fusion of colour and texture features and two frozen deep feature sets, and compare it with single-feature baselines, early fusion and a fine-tuned network under session-grouped validation;", "(iii) build FreshCheck by late fusion of colour and texture features with frozen deep features taken at several network depths and image scales, and compare it with single-feature baselines, fusion of final-layer features and a fine-tuned network under session-grouped validation;")
rep('Figure 1. How FreshCheck reaches a decision', "Figure 1. How FreshCheck reaches a decision, shown for one half-fresh red-meat photograph with models trained without its capture session. The photograph is described by colour and texture features, by MobileNetV3 features and by ResNet-50 features taken at three depths from whole-image and local views (only part of each is shown). Nine logistic regressions, one per feature set, each give class probabilities (shown averaged within the three groups), and all nine are averaged. Here the colour models alone would have called the meat fresh; the average calls it half-fresh. If the top averaged probability is below the threshold τ, the photograph is referred to staff. τ is chosen on training photographs only, as the smallest value at which no more than 1% of spoiled photographs are accepted as fresh.", key='caption')
for b in C:
    if b.get('file')=='fig1_pipeline_v2.png': b['file']='fig1_pipeline_v3.png'
    if b.get('file')=='fig5_results.png': b['file']='fig4_results_v2.png'
    if b.get('file')=='fig4_examples.png': b['file']='fig5_examples_v2.png'
rep('FreshCheck (Fig. 1) combines three feature sets',
"FreshCheck (Fig. 1) describes a photograph with nine feature sets in three groups. The colour group holds two sets computed at 224 × 224 pixels: a colour and texture set (50 values: marginal HSV histograms with 18, 8 and 8 bins, a 10-bin histogram of uniform local binary patterns, and the mean and standard deviation of each Lab channel) and a fine colour set (85 values: five percentiles of each Lab channel, an 8 × 8 joint histogram of a* and b*, and Lab means and standard deviations). The second group is the 1,024-dimensional pooled output of MobileNetV3-Small (Howard et al., 2019). The third group comes from ResNet-50 (He et al., 2016; timm weights resnet50.a1_in1k; Wightman, 2019). Spoilage shows first as changes in surface colour, sheen and fine texture, which are encoded in the middle of a network, whereas its final layer summarises the whole scene, including the tray and background that identify a capture session. We therefore took the globally pooled outputs of residual stages 2, 3 and 4 (512, 1,024 and 2,048 values), each from two views: a whole-image view (the image at 224 × 224 pixels and its mirror image, averaged) and a local view (the image resized to 448 × 448 pixels and cut into four 224 × 224 patches, averaged), giving six sets. All networks were pretrained on ImageNet and used frozen, so the system runs on an ordinary computer without a graphics card. Each feature set feeds its own standardised, L2-regularised logistic regression (C = 1), and FreshCheck averages the nine predicted class-probability vectors (late fusion). All nine sets were used on every dataset without selection, except that the 50-value colour and texture set was not computed for fruit.")
sub('If the largest averaged probability', "(0.50 for red meat and 0.74 for chicken)", "(0.50 for red meat and 0.71 for chicken)")
rep('FreshCheck was compared with each feature set on its own',
"FreshCheck was compared with single feature sets (colour and texture, MobileNetV3, and the final pooled ResNet-50 output that is the usual transfer-learning feature), each with the same logistic regression; with final-layer fusion, the average of those three models; and with early fusion, in which those three feature sets were concatenated and fed to one logistic regression. Three ablations show where any gain comes from: the six ResNet-50 sets alone, the whole-image views alone and the local views alone. To check that freezing the networks does not explain the results, MobileNetV3-Small was also fine-tuned end to end on the red-meat set: 160 × 160 inputs, four epochs, AdamW (learning rate 5 × 10⁻⁴, weight decay 10⁻⁴, cosine schedule), batch size 32, random-resized-crop (70–100% of the area) and horizontal-flip augmentation, and a centre crop at test time. Training ran on a two-core CPU, so fine-tuning used three fold assignments (seeds 0–2) for image-level folds and capture-order blocks of 100, and one for random groups of 100. We also tested two further feature extractors, a bank of Gabor filters modelled on the primary visual cortex and a simulated eight-qubit quantum-kernel classifier; neither improved the fusion, and they are reported in the Supplementary material.")
sub('To show how much the validation design matters', "For block sizes of 20 and 100 we drew", "The same random-grouping control with blocks of 100 was applied to FreshCheck. For block sizes of 20 and 100 we drew")
sub('To mimic installation at a new site', "The single-feature and fine-tuned models were tested in the same way.", "Final-layer fusion and the single-feature and fine-tuned models were tested in the same way.")
rep('Under session-grouped validation FreshCheck was the most accurate model',
"Under session-grouped validation FreshCheck was the most accurate model on the two meat datasets and matched the best on fruit (Table 5; Fig. 4a). On red meat it reached 95.2% ± 0.6%, against 87.4% for the final ResNet-50 layer, 90.3% for final-layer fusion and 89.8% for early fusion, so the errors of final-layer fusion were halved (9.7% to 4.8%). On chicken it reached 98.4% ± 0.1% (final-layer fusion 97.2%), and the share of spoiled samples accepted as fresh fell from 3.2% to 1.8%. On fruit, where the baselines were already close together, the gain was within the variation between fold assignments (97.4% ± 0.2% against 97.2% ± 0.3%).")
i=[k for k,b in enumerate(C) if b.get('text','').startswith('Under session-grouped validation FreshCheck was the most accurate')][0]
C.insert(i+1, {'k':'p','text':"Mid-level features carried most of the gain. A single logistic regression on the stage-2 ResNet-50 output reached 94.7% on red meat and 97.7% on chicken, 7.3 and 1.6 points above the final layer of the same network, and accuracy on red meat fell with depth (94.7%, 92.6% and 88.5% for stages 2, 3 and 4 from the whole-image view). Local views helped the deeper stages (stage 4: 92.3% from local views against 88.5%). The six ResNet-50 sets alone gave 95.1% and 98.4%, and the whole-image and local views alone gave 94.3% and 94.2% on red meat, so depth mattered more than view and the two views were complementary. With image-level folds FreshCheck scored 99.8% on red meat, 98.8% on chicken and 97.9% on fruit; the red-meat figure equals the best published values for this dataset (99.66–99.78%; Külcü & Balpetek Külcü, 2026), but only the session-grouped figure describes unseen material. Even so, FreshCheck narrowed the gap between the two protocols on red meat from 8.7 points (final-layer fusion: 99.0% against 90.3%) to 4.6 points. The gap did not close: 20 random groupings of the same size scored 99.6–99.8% on red meat and 98.5–99.0% on chicken, above the session-grouped result in every case (p = 0.048 for both)."})
for b in C:
    if b.get('k')=='table' and b.get('n')==5:
        b['title']='FreshCheck and baseline models under session-grouped five-fold cross-validation (%; mean ± SD over five fold assignments; red meat and fruit three classes, chicken two)'
        b['rows']=[['Model','Red meat accuracy','Red meat spoiled-as-fresh','Chicken accuracy','Chicken spoiled-as-fresh','Fruit accuracy','Fruit spoiled-as-fresh'],
 ['Colour and texture','83.5 ± 0.7','0.8','92.4 ± 0.3','8.2','—','—'],
 ['MobileNetV3 (frozen)','86.9 ± 0.8','0.1','95.0 ± 0.2','5.2','96.8 ± 0.4','0.7'],
 ['ResNet-50, final layer','87.4 ± 0.9','0.1','96.1 ± 0.1','3.6','96.7 ± 0.3','0.8'],
 ['ResNet-50, stage 2 (whole image)','94.7 ± 0.3','0.0','97.7 ± 0.1','2.9','97.1 ± 0.2','0.7'],
 ['Early fusion (final layers)','89.8 ± 0.7','0.0','97.1 ± 0.2','3.0','97.1 ± 0.2','0.6'],
 ['Final-layer fusion','90.3 ± 0.9','0.0','97.2 ± 0.2','3.2','97.2 ± 0.3','0.5'],
 ['Ablation: six ResNet-50 sets only','95.1 ± 0.4','0.0','98.4 ± 0.1','1.6','97.3 ± 0.2','0.6'],
 ['Ablation: whole-image views only','94.3 ± 0.7','0.0','98.2 ± 0.2','1.9','97.4 ± 0.1','0.3'],
 ['Ablation: local views only','94.2 ± 0.7','0.0','98.0 ± 0.1','2.4','97.5 ± 0.2','0.5'],
 ['FreshCheck (multi-level fusion)','95.2 ± 0.6','0.0','98.4 ± 0.1','1.8','97.4 ± 0.2','0.4'],
 ['FreshCheck with refer rule: coverage','99.4 ± 0.1','—','96.8 ± 0.3','—','99.7 ± 0.3','—'],
 ['FreshCheck with refer rule: accuracy on decided images','95.4 ± 0.5','0.0','99.3 ± 0.0','0.8','97.6 ± 0.2','0.3']]
    if b.get('k')=='table' and b.get('n')==6:
        b['title']='FreshCheck on a new dataset with labelled local images (binary fresh vs spoiled; %; mean ± SD over 20 random draws of local capture sessions)'
        b['rows']=[['Direction and local sessions per class','Local images','Accuracy, other dataset + local','Spoiled-as-fresh, other dataset + local','Accuracy, local images only','Spoiled-as-fresh, local images only'],
 ['Red meat → chicken, none','0','49.0','100.0','—','—'],
 ['Red meat → chicken, 1','13','72.1 ± 10.8','39.6','78.0 ± 9.5','21.0'],
 ['Red meat → chicken, 2','30','81.2 ± 8.3','24.8','84.2 ± 7.9','19.9'],
 ['Red meat → chicken, 5','79','93.7 ± 3.1','7.9','94.3 ± 2.5','7.0'],
 ['Red meat → chicken, 10','181','96.5 ± 0.6','3.7','96.7 ± 0.6','3.4'],
 ['Chicken → red meat, none','0','66.5','38.1','—','—'],
 ['Chicken → red meat, 1','32','83.2 ± 7.3','23.2','94.2 ± 7.6','9.1'],
 ['Chicken → red meat, 2','70','91.0 ± 6.1','9.2','98.3 ± 2.7','1.9'],
 ['Chicken → red meat, 5','168','96.9 ± 1.1','2.3','99.6 ± 0.3','0.1'],
 ['Chicken → red meat, 10','346','98.6 ± 0.7','0.5','99.8 ± 0.1','0.0']]
rep('Note to Table 5', "Note to Table 5: 'final layer' is the pooled output normally used for transfer learning; 'stage 2' is the pooled output of the second residual stage. Fruit models did not use the 50-value colour and texture set. Fold assignments were generated separately from those in Tables 3 and 4, so baseline values can differ from them by up to 0.2 points.")
rep('Figure 4. Performance of FreshCheck', "Figure 4. Performance of FreshCheck. (a) Accuracy of single feature sets, final-layer fusion and FreshCheck (multi-level fusion) under session-grouped five-fold cross-validation on red meat (three classes) and chicken breast (two classes); error bars show the standard deviation over five fold assignments. (b) Accuracy of FreshCheck on a new dataset as labelled local images are added: trained on the other meat dataset plus local images (solid) and on the local images alone (dashed); error bars show the standard deviation over 20 random draws of local sessions.", key='caption')
rep('The refer rule mattered most where errors were uncertain',
"The refer rule mattered where errors were uncertain. On chicken, the thresholds chosen on the training folds lay mostly between 0.65 and 0.74; FreshCheck then gave a class to 96.8% of images, with 99.3% accuracy on those, and referred 3.6% of spoiled and 2.8% of fresh images to staff. The share of all spoiled chicken images accepted as fresh fell from 1.8% to 0.8%, meeting the 1% target set on training data. With final-layer fusion the same target required referring 8.1% of images, so the stronger features cut the staff workload by more than half. On red meat, FreshCheck accepted no spoiled image as fresh even without the rule, so the threshold stayed at 0.50 and 0.6% of images were referred. The remaining red-meat errors were confident confusions between fresh and half-fresh in sessions the model had not seen, which a confidence threshold cannot catch. On fruit, 0.3% of images were referred. Fig. 5 shows held-out examples of correct decisions, referrals and errors.")
rep('Without local images, no model transferred',
"Without local images, no model transferred between the two meat datasets. FreshCheck trained on red meat reached 49.0% on chicken and accepted every spoiled chicken image as fresh; trained on chicken, it reached 66.5% on red meat and accepted 38.1% of spoiled red meat as fresh. Final-layer fusion gave 49.8% and 54.8%, and the single-feature models did no better: frozen MobileNetV3 reached 58.2% and 64.4%, the final ResNet-50 layer 50.1% and 49.5%, colour and texture features 47.8% and 42.7%, and the fine-tuned network 50.4% and 42.4%.")
rep('Labelled local images restored performance quickly',
"Labelled local images restored performance quickly (Table 6; Fig. 4b). With one capture session per class from the new dataset (about 13 chicken or 32 red-meat images), accuracy rose to 72.1% on chicken and 83.2% on red meat; with two sessions (30 and 70 images) to 81.2% and 91.0%; and with five (79 and 168 images) to 93.7% and 96.9%, while the share of spoiled samples accepted as fresh fell to 7.9% and 2.3%. The other dataset contributed nothing beyond the local images themselves: trained on the local images alone, FreshCheck did as well on chicken (78.0%, 84.2%, 94.3% and 96.7%) and better on red meat (94.2%, 98.3%, 99.6% and 99.8%). Variation between draws was large with one or two sessions (standard deviations of about 6–11 points) and small with five or more.")
rep('The application loads the trained models', "The application loads the trained models in a few seconds and returns a decision for a photograph in about 0.35 s (median over 20 images; 90th percentile 0.42 s) on a two-core CPU without a graphics card, including feature extraction by both networks. Because the released models were trained on all images of the two public datasets, the application illustrates the workflow but does not provide an independent test; Sections 3.3–3.5 give the held-out performance a new site should expect.")
rep('Under validation that keeps capture sessions apart, combining',
"The main gain came from where in the network the features were taken. The final layer of a pretrained network, the default in transfer learning and in the studies cited above, summarises the whole scene, and under session-grouped validation it was the weakest ResNet-50 feature on red meat (87.4%); the second stage of the same network reached 94.7%. Mid-level features describe colour, sheen and fine surface texture, which is where spoilage shows, and carry less of the cut, tray and background that mark a capture session. Fusing several depths, two views and colour features halved the errors of final-layer fusion on red meat and narrowed the gap between image-level and session-grouped accuracy from 8.7 to 4.6 points. This comparison could only be made under grouped validation: with image-level folds every one of these features scored above 98% on red meat and the differences between them disappeared. The refer rule turns the classifier into a check that can be set to a safety target. On chicken it met a 1% target for spoiled samples accepted as fresh by referring about 3% of images, a workload a person at receiving can carry. Where errors come from sessions unlike any in training, as in red meat and in transfer, the model is confidently wrong and abstention cannot help; such errors have to be prevented by local data and checked by local validation.")
sub('Removing duplicates by hash took out almost none', "The drop was larger for deep features (11.5 points for MobileNetV3) than for colour and texture features (6.3 points), consistent with deep features picking up session context.", "The drop was larger for final-layer deep features (11.5 points for MobileNetV3) than for colour and texture features (6.3 points) or for multi-level fusion (4.6 points), consistent with final-layer features picking up session context.")
sub('The transfer results set the conditions', "the models here passed most spoiled samples as fresh when moved between two meat datasets", "the models here passed 38–100% of spoiled samples as fresh when moved between two meat datasets")
sub('The transfer results set the conditions', "(here 80–170 images)", "(here about 80–170 images, giving 94–99%)")
sub('File numbers stand in for capture sessions', "Fine-tuning used a short schedule because of computing limits.", "Fine-tuning used a short schedule because of computing limits, and the feature depths and views were fixed in advance rather than tuned, so other choices may do better.")
rep('This study developed FreshCheck', "This study developed FreshCheck, an image classifier that labels food as fresh or spoiled and refers uncertain images to staff, and validated it on public red-meat, chicken-breast and fruit datasets after screening them for duplicates and capture sessions. Screening showed that image-level validation overstated red-meat accuracy by up to 11.5 points and that public prepared-dish collections consisted largely of copies. Under session-grouped validation, fusing mid-level and final-layer features from whole-image and local views raised accuracy from 90.3% to 95.2% on red meat and from 97.2% to 98.4% on chicken, and on chicken the refer rule cut spoiled samples accepted as fresh to 0.8% while deciding 96.8% of images. No model transferred between the two meat datasets, but about five labelled local capture sessions per class restored 94–97% accuracy. Image-based freshness checks should be validated by session, judged by the rate at which spoiled food is accepted, and calibrated with local images before use.")
json.dump(C, open(P,'w'), ensure_ascii=False, indent=1)
txt=[b for b in C if b['k'] in ('p','h1','h2')]; print('words', sum(len(b['text'].split()) for b in txt), 'abstract', len([b for b in C if b.get('abstract')][0]['text'].split()))
